WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.735
Our first guest tonight
is an 11-time

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.937
Grammy-winning musician
and one of the biggest stars

00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:06.239
in the world.

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:08.707
Her album,
"Red (Taylor's Version)"...

00:00:08.707 --> 00:00:10.376
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-...is out tomorrow.

00:00:10.376 --> 00:00:12.812
She's also returning
to "SNL" this week

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:15.281
as musical guest along
with host Jonathan Majors.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:16.849
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:19.152
the one, the only...
Taylor Swift, everybody.

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:21.354
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:30.997 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:40.606 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:42.709
Welcome back to the show.

00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:44.143
-Thanks for having me back.

00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:45.945
-We love having you
in the building, especially

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:49.315
when it means we get to see you
on "SNL" this coming Saturday.

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.150
Your fifth time
as a musical guest.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.719
-Yes. What a dream.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.086
-And -- And a one-time host.

00:00:54.086 --> 00:00:55.788
An incredible host
back in the day.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:57.156
-Thank you!

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:58.557
-And I'm wondering. You --
-Back when I was teen.

00:00:58.557 --> 00:00:59.892
-That's crazy
how young you were.

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:01.293
-Yeah, I was -- Yeah.

00:01:01.293 --> 00:01:03.129
-Because you're young now,
and that was a long time ago.

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:05.164
-That is so -- Thank you.
-You're very welcome.

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:07.700
I can say it honestly
because I've also aged.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.068
-What a -- What a friend.
No, you haven't.

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:10.570
-Thank you very much.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.238
-That's -- Those are lies.
Don't say lies.

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:13.539
-Having been a one-time --

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:15.475
and a first-time host,
obviously, when you hosted,

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:17.109
do you have any advice
for Jonathan,

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:19.045
your first-time host this week?

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:22.515
-Well, I -- I met him,
and I just want to say

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:24.817
he seems like one of
the coolest people on Earth.

00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:26.919
-Yeah.
-I think that everyone

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:29.688
needs to watch and just like --
He's amazing.

00:01:29.688 --> 00:01:31.757
I mean, his work is
obviously so incredible,

00:01:31.757 --> 00:01:33.860
and I'm a big fan,
but meeting him,

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.528
you just get such a good --

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:37.630
Very real, very, like --

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.133
abnormally talented
normal personality.

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.434
-Yes.

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:43.403
-Those people
are unicorn-special.

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:45.839
-Yes, it's so -- it's a very --
very nice combination.

00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:47.640
-Those are special,
special people.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.409
So, like, you know,
I -- I don't --

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:51.677
You know, what's weird
is I didn't get any advice

00:01:51.677 --> 00:01:54.614
from anyone when I was hosting,

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:57.550
which is actually weird,
because I was like 19.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:58.684
-Yeah.

00:01:58.684 --> 00:02:01.220
-So I should've
been given advice.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.522
-Yeah. Real ball drop
on our -- our end.

00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:04.691
-Right?
-Yeah.

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:06.125
-There should have been,
like, a tip -- like --

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:08.895
like a number people call
to give me advice.

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:10.529
But, no,
it's -- it's -- you know.

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:12.231
I think it's just
gonna be amazing.

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:15.401
I'm so happy to get to be there,
to perform and to sing.

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:19.939
You know, I -- I haven't played
with my band in years.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:22.275
And we're all like --
We all find it hard

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:24.610
not to, like, cry
that we get to play together.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:25.945
Like, we're all like,

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.780
"Did you guys make it through
that last pass?"

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.683
We're in rehearsals, just,
like, embarrassing ourselves.

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:32.718
So it's just a dream
to get to do it.

00:02:32.718 --> 00:02:34.687
-Well, I think
it must be emotional

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:37.222
just to get back together,
but also, part of the emotion

00:02:37.222 --> 00:02:40.360
is you're singing songs from --
You know, you're re-recording.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.162
This is your second re-record.
-Yes.

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:46.132
I feel like -- I know
that everybody has busy lives,

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:49.902
so I -- I do feel the need to,
like, explain what I'm doing

00:02:49.902 --> 00:02:51.938
because it's not normal.

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:53.539
-Yeah.

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:56.742
-Basically, like, music --

00:02:56.742 --> 00:02:58.311
I've always wanted
to own my own music

00:02:58.311 --> 00:03:00.078
since I started making music,
and if --

00:03:00.078 --> 00:03:02.281
You probably don't know this,
but most of your favorite

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:05.218
artists do not own their work.

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:09.521
The music industry is, uh --
Eh, you know?

00:03:09.521 --> 00:03:11.023
Certain corners of it.

00:03:11.023 --> 00:03:13.325
But I think that there was --

00:03:13.325 --> 00:03:18.197
There was something that
happened years ago where I --

00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:19.732
I made it very clear

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:21.634
that I wanted to be able
to buy my music.

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:24.236
That opportunity
was not given me,

00:03:24.236 --> 00:03:26.172
and it was sold
to somebody else.

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:29.808
And so I just figured, I was the
one who made this music first.

00:03:29.808 --> 00:03:31.677
I can just make it again.

00:03:31.677 --> 00:03:33.446
-Yeah.
-So that's what we're doing.

00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:34.947
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:37.784
-I have to say...

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:39.451
-So that's what we're doing.

00:03:39.451 --> 00:03:41.187
So when something says

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:44.290
"(Taylor's Version)"
next to it,

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.392
that means I own it,
which is exciting.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.661
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:52.231
-It's -- It's quite -- It's
quite a clever loophole, Taylor.

00:03:52.231 --> 00:03:54.133
-Oh, thank you.
-I really tip my cap.

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:55.601
-Oh, thank you.

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:58.971
It's -- You know, it's
also interesting to kind of

00:03:58.971 --> 00:04:02.341
go back and relive
this nostalgia with fans

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:05.011
who are the reason
why I get to do this

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.746
and why I get to, like, sit here

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:09.782
and, like, have
this lovely chat with you.

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:12.351
Going back and -- and reliving
these things with the fans.

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:14.953
And this time around,
I get to really --

00:04:14.953 --> 00:04:16.922
I get to do things
that I know they wish

00:04:16.922 --> 00:04:18.257
I would have done
the first time.

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:19.625
-That's really good.

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:21.527
-Because I'm always listening
and I'm always lurking.

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:23.028
And I'm always listening

00:04:23.028 --> 00:04:25.197
to their opinions
and their theories.

00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:26.866
And what --
You know, they'll -- they --

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:29.569
They will let me know which
songs should have been singles.

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:31.771
-Yeah.
-They let me know which songs

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:35.074
did not get videos
and should have gotten videos!

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:37.076
And so I just like --
You know what?

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:38.411
Like, I'm listening

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:41.481
and I'm making the videos
and I'm doing the things.

00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:43.348
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-That is really cool.

00:04:43.348 --> 00:04:47.486
I want to ask -- I mean, I have
emotional connections to songs

00:04:47.486 --> 00:04:49.822
I listen to for the first time
ten years ago.

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:51.724
I can only imagine
what it's like for you,

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:53.225
having written them.

00:04:53.225 --> 00:04:56.996
Like, is it -- is it cathartic
to go back and re-record them?

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:59.298
Or does it -- When they're
about painful things,

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:01.200
do you, like, feel it
all over again?

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.702
-I mean, I think -- for --

00:05:03.702 --> 00:05:06.138
One thing that I noticed
about this --

00:05:06.138 --> 00:05:08.073
just this week in general

00:05:08.073 --> 00:05:12.578
is that I think back to
the release week of "Red,"

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:15.380
which is the album
that is, you know...

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:17.450
Just in case
anybody missed it.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:18.750
So this originally came out --

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.586
-This is it. It's this.
It's Taylor's Version.

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:22.654
-Yeah, it came out originally
about a decade ago.

00:05:22.654 --> 00:05:28.628
And I was 22, and that release
week was so stressful

00:05:28.628 --> 00:05:31.063
because nobody's heard
any of the music.

00:05:31.063 --> 00:05:34.033
There are like 14 different
genre's on this album.

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:36.803
It's a real patchwork quilt
of genre.

00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:38.671
I was really experimenting.

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:41.207
-A lot of 22-year-olds are going
to bounce around on genres.

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:43.876
-Hopefully. Yeah, you know.
That's what we want for them.

00:05:43.876 --> 00:05:46.412
-Absolutely.
-But I think that --

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:49.181
I was so focused on, like,
"Is anyone going to like it?"

00:05:49.181 --> 00:05:50.449
And then I was also, like,

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:52.385
at the time, like,
honestly, really sad.

00:05:52.385 --> 00:05:54.654
'Cause I had actually
gone through,

00:05:54.654 --> 00:05:58.590
you know, the stuff
that I had sung about.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:01.660
But this time, I'm just like --
I got, like -- I have, like --

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:04.496
got sunglasses on
and, like, a mojito

00:06:04.496 --> 00:06:07.232
and just like --
It's chill this time.

00:06:07.232 --> 00:06:10.569
It's really nice to be able to
put this album out and, like --

00:06:10.569 --> 00:06:12.705
and not be sad, not be, like,

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:15.340
taking breaks in between
interviews to cry.

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:18.444
It's very -- I'm telling you,
it's much better this way.

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:20.579
-Yeah.
-Much better.

00:06:20.579 --> 00:06:22.949
-We -- I think we're all happy.
-Yeah.

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:24.016
-With that said...

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:26.519
-You get some time passing,
you know?

00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:28.520
-Yeah.
-You're -- You're reliving

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:30.957
your experience from your 20s
when you're in your 30s.

00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:32.992
It's really the way to do it.
-Yeah.

00:06:32.992 --> 00:06:35.961
I wonder if --
if there are people who might

00:06:35.961 --> 00:06:39.164
think that they were the one
you were singing about,

00:06:39.164 --> 00:06:43.268
if it's easier or far, far worse
for them ten years later.

00:06:43.268 --> 00:06:45.371
-I haven't thought about their
experience, to be honest.

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:48.741
-I think that's the --
that's the biggest burn.

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:50.042
[ Laughter ]

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:51.544
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:51.544 --> 00:06:54.980
I think there's nothing
they'd rather hear less.

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.817
[ Laughs ] Hey. I have so many
more questions for you.

00:06:57.817 --> 00:07:00.586
We'll be right back with more
from Taylor Swift.

00:07:00.586 --> 00:07:03.355
Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Taylor Swift.

00:07:03.355 --> 00:07:05.557
And we were talking
during the break.

00:07:05.557 --> 00:07:08.394
Some very exciting
collaborations that you are now

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:11.163
able to do because you're
re-recording these songs --

00:07:11.163 --> 00:07:13.666
Chris Stapleton, Ed Sheeran,
Phoebe Bridgers.

00:07:13.666 --> 00:07:15.133
Is that --
-Yeah.

00:07:15.133 --> 00:07:17.202
[ Cheers and applause ]
-It must be so nice to

00:07:17.202 --> 00:07:20.205
have a wish list, and then
reach out to these people.

00:07:20.205 --> 00:07:24.443
-It really, really was.
Essentially, there are songs

00:07:24.443 --> 00:07:28.014
on this album that I've called
"from the vault" tracks,

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:30.516
because in my mind,
there's a metaphorical vault

00:07:30.516 --> 00:07:34.120
of songs that I love
that I've written --

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:38.057
I write a lot of songs for
albums, and then I pare it down.

00:07:38.057 --> 00:07:40.926
But these were songs that
didn't make it on the album

00:07:40.926 --> 00:07:43.162
because I wanted to save them
for the next album.

00:07:43.162 --> 00:07:44.930
And then, it turned out,
the next album was, like,

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.667
a whole different thing,
and so they get left behind.

00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:50.303
And you always think back on
these songs, and you're like,

00:07:50.303 --> 00:07:53.739
"What would have happened?
I wish people could hear this."

00:07:53.739 --> 00:07:56.208
But it belongs
in that moment in time.

00:07:56.208 --> 00:08:00.912
So now that I get to go back
and revisit my old work,

00:08:00.912 --> 00:08:06.819
I've dug up those songs
from the crypt they were in,

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:10.556
and I have, like --
I've reached out to artists

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:13.391
that I love and said, "Do you
want to sing this with me?"

00:08:13.391 --> 00:08:16.596
You know, Phoebe Bridgers
is one of my favorite artists

00:08:16.596 --> 00:08:18.931
in the world.
I just think she's --

00:08:18.931 --> 00:08:22.235
Like, if she sings it,
I will listen to it.

00:08:22.235 --> 00:08:23.769
[ Laughter ]
It's --

00:08:23.769 --> 00:08:26.205
I just love her voice,
and then I also love

00:08:26.205 --> 00:08:29.074
that she's a very funny person.
-Do these people believe it

00:08:29.074 --> 00:08:31.610
when they get a phone call,
saying, "Hi, it's Taylor Swift.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:32.778
I want you
to sing a song with me?"

00:08:32.778 --> 00:08:34.413
-Well, I try not
to cold call people.

00:08:34.413 --> 00:08:36.215
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:37.983
That's a good instinct, yeah.
-It can go very bad.

00:08:37.983 --> 00:08:42.554
-Sure.
-But, you know, I do send, like,

00:08:42.554 --> 00:08:47.926
a very long text that
I've crafted over many days.

00:08:47.926 --> 00:08:49.795
And I'll send the song,
because I don't want them

00:08:49.795 --> 00:08:52.097
to ever feel pressured
to say yes to something

00:08:52.097 --> 00:08:54.966
creatively if it doesn't gel
with what they want to do.

00:08:54.966 --> 00:08:57.503
So, with Phoebe, I reached out,

00:08:57.503 --> 00:08:59.304
and I sent her this song
called "Nothing New,"

00:08:59.304 --> 00:09:02.808
which I wrote when I was 22.
And it's really, really

00:09:02.808 --> 00:09:05.411
special to me
because it was the first time

00:09:05.411 --> 00:09:07.413
I was not a shiny new artist.

00:09:07.413 --> 00:09:11.249
I was on my fourth album,
and I felt --

00:09:11.249 --> 00:09:12.885
Like, I think this happens
to a lot of artists,

00:09:12.885 --> 00:09:14.887
where they have
their breakthrough moment.

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:17.156
And then, the moment after that
is really hard for them

00:09:17.156 --> 00:09:19.791
because they're just
not getting the same --

00:09:19.791 --> 00:09:23.528
It's like the first time
you walk into a room at a party

00:09:23.528 --> 00:09:26.365
and everyone's like,
"Oh, Kevin's here!"

00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:28.801
When you have your breakthrough
moment, like, "Kevin!"

00:09:28.801 --> 00:09:32.504
And then, the next time, you
walk in, they're like, "'Sup?"

00:09:32.504 --> 00:09:33.773
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:33.773 --> 00:09:36.308
-And then, the third time
you walk in, they're like,

00:09:36.308 --> 00:09:39.878
"Um, so what do you want
to do tomorrow?"

00:09:39.878 --> 00:09:41.813
and they're just talking.
And you're like,

00:09:41.813 --> 00:09:43.615
"But I'm here,
and you used to --"

00:09:43.615 --> 00:09:46.218
So I was dealing with
that moment in my life,

00:09:46.218 --> 00:09:48.787
and so that song's
really special to me.

00:09:48.787 --> 00:09:52.491
And I sent it to Phoebe
and said it would mean

00:09:52.491 --> 00:09:54.093
the world to me
if you would do this as a duet.

00:09:54.093 --> 00:09:55.660
'Cause I really wanted
another female artist

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:58.330
who I loved to sing it with me,
because I think it has a very

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:02.133
female-artist perspective, that
we go through that experience.

00:10:02.133 --> 00:10:04.603
And her response was,

00:10:04.603 --> 00:10:06.372
"I've been waiting for this text
my entire life."

00:10:06.372 --> 00:10:07.773
[ Laughter ]

00:10:07.773 --> 00:10:11.444
It was like, "Yes!"
-That's a very --

00:10:11.444 --> 00:10:12.845
I'm sure
she was waiting for that.

00:10:12.845 --> 00:10:15.080
I think a lot of people
are waiting and excited

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:16.882
for the fact
that you have recorded --

00:10:16.882 --> 00:10:19.618
There's a 10-minute version
of a song --

00:10:19.618 --> 00:10:24.089
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:24.089 --> 00:10:25.457
I mean,
I-I just want to say.

00:10:25.457 --> 00:10:26.525
-That was
a really nice reaction.

00:10:26.525 --> 00:10:28.494
-I haven't heard a gasp
like that...

00:10:28.494 --> 00:10:29.929
-That was great.
-...since I said,

00:10:29.929 --> 00:10:31.163
"Now it's time
for 'A Closer Look.'"

00:10:31.163 --> 00:10:36.268
-That was very powerful.
[ Laughter ]

00:10:36.268 --> 00:10:38.304
-So, tell us about --
But this was --

00:10:38.304 --> 00:10:39.905
So you've always
had this version.

00:10:39.905 --> 00:10:42.475
-Yes. This is the original
thing that I wrote.

00:10:42.475 --> 00:10:45.644
So, there's a song called
"All Too Well"

00:10:45.644 --> 00:10:49.314
that was never a single.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:49.314 --> 00:10:50.882
You guys are really --

00:10:50.882 --> 00:10:52.818
This is very nice,
what you're doing out there.

00:10:52.818 --> 00:10:54.552
[ Laughter ]

00:10:54.552 --> 00:10:57.222
This song was never a single,
it never had a video,

00:10:57.222 --> 00:10:59.858
and somehow,
the fans just turned it into

00:10:59.858 --> 00:11:02.227
the song from this album.

00:11:02.227 --> 00:11:04.996
And when I --
I used to get so sad

00:11:04.996 --> 00:11:06.432
when I would sing it
that I could barely, like,

00:11:06.432 --> 00:11:09.134
get through the song.
And then, over time, I realized

00:11:09.134 --> 00:11:12.204
the fans were just, like,
screaming the words back to me

00:11:12.204 --> 00:11:15.640
so loudly that it made it
a very joyful experience for me

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:17.109
to sing this song.
And since --

00:11:17.109 --> 00:11:19.411
It has been a joyful thing
for me to sing.

00:11:19.411 --> 00:11:21.680
I play it all the time
in concert.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:25.017
And it's just so fun
to hear them scream the words.

00:11:25.017 --> 00:11:28.421
So this song originally was --

00:11:28.421 --> 00:11:30.455
It's a very long song, as it is,

00:11:30.455 --> 00:11:33.059
but it was originally
10 minutes long.

00:11:33.059 --> 00:11:36.561
And I just kept --
I was just like --

00:11:36.561 --> 00:11:39.465
[ Laughter ]
I couldn't stop.

00:11:39.465 --> 00:11:42.301
But I was like, "Oh, you got to
fit something on an album,

00:11:42.301 --> 00:11:45.303
so that's unreasonable for it
to be 10 minutes long."

00:11:45.303 --> 00:11:48.207
And so I had to cut out certain
verses and parts of the bridge

00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:52.077
and lots of different things
that I really loved.

00:11:52.077 --> 00:11:55.547
Left some of my favorite lines
on the cutting-room floor,

00:11:55.547 --> 00:11:57.750
and I'm really happy
that people get to hear them.

00:11:57.750 --> 00:11:59.551
I'm so proud of
this version of it.

00:11:59.551 --> 00:12:01.586
I think this version
is the version of the song

00:12:01.586 --> 00:12:02.988
that was meant to be heard.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:12:02.988 --> 00:12:08.761
-That is really thrilling.
And then, on top of all that,

00:12:08.761 --> 00:12:11.530
there's a short film
that you wrote.

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:12.831
-Yeah.
-That you directed.

00:12:12.831 --> 00:12:15.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:12:15.067 --> 00:12:18.403
Two fantastic young actors --
Dylan and Sadie,

00:12:18.403 --> 00:12:20.405
who I believe --
I hope this is correct --

00:12:20.405 --> 00:12:21.774
also huge fans of yours.

00:12:21.774 --> 00:12:23.775
It must have been a big deal
for them to do this.

00:12:23.775 --> 00:12:25.277
-It was really wonderful

00:12:25.277 --> 00:12:27.078
working with Sadie Sink
and Dylan O'Brien.

00:12:27.078 --> 00:12:28.780
They're just absolutely --

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:32.351
I'm just blown away by what
they did in this short film.

00:12:32.351 --> 00:12:37.756
I wrote this short film
and wanted to direct it,

00:12:37.756 --> 00:12:41.660
and the only two people
that I imagined playing

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:45.130
the two characters --
it was Sadie and Dylan.

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:47.533
If Sadie --

00:12:47.533 --> 00:12:49.000
She was the first one I went to.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.237
And if Sadie had said no,

00:12:51.237 --> 00:12:52.671
I don't think
I would have made it.

00:12:52.671 --> 00:12:53.905
I don't think
I would've made the film.

00:12:53.905 --> 00:12:54.939
I think I would've
just been like,

00:12:54.939 --> 00:12:57.076
"This is a sign,"
like, you know.

00:12:57.076 --> 00:13:01.246
But she --
I do like working with friends,

00:13:01.246 --> 00:13:04.883
or people who I think would be
excited about working with me.

00:13:04.883 --> 00:13:07.686
I don't really want
to have to convince someone.

00:13:07.686 --> 00:13:09.922
I'm not very interested
in that at all.

00:13:09.922 --> 00:13:12.791
If somebody -- I've never
made a short film before.

00:13:12.791 --> 00:13:15.860
I've directed some music videos.
But I kinda needed to reach out

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:18.864
to people who were like --
would maybe believe

00:13:18.864 --> 00:13:22.834
that I would be capable of it,
and Sadie just --

00:13:22.834 --> 00:13:25.671
Sadie got it immediately.
Dylan was like, "Yep."

00:13:25.671 --> 00:13:28.440
And I watch everything he's in.
I'm a huge fan of his.

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:31.677
And both of them, they just --
they went out,

00:13:31.677 --> 00:13:34.579
and they just left it all on the
field there in that short film.

00:13:34.579 --> 00:13:37.783
They really just
acted their asses off.

00:13:37.783 --> 00:13:41.686
-Well, it's terrific.
And I can only imagine

00:13:41.686 --> 00:13:44.456
what the next couple of days are
gonna be like for your fans.

00:13:44.456 --> 00:13:45.757
You have done them --
-I hope good.

00:13:45.757 --> 00:13:46.858
I hope good --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:13:46.858 --> 00:13:50.862
-I feel very confidant
about that.

00:13:50.862 --> 00:13:52.564
It's so lovely to see you again.
-You're lovely.

00:13:52.564 --> 00:13:55.400
-Thanks for being here.
You guys, that's Taylor Swift.

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:57.002
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:13:57.002 --> 00:13:59.605
"Red (Taylor's Version)"
is out tomorrow.

